# Keep the fingerprint reader powered.
#
# The Synaptics sensor (06cb:00fc) autosuspends after two seconds idle and does
# not come back reliably: it drops off the USB bus entirely, and fprintd then
# fails with "USB error ... No such device (it may have been disconnected)".
#
# The visible symptom is that hyprlock shows no fingerprint prompt at all.
# hyprlock renders $FPRINTPROMPT only once it has claimed a device, so a
# suspended reader produces a bare "Enter password" rather than an error —
# which reads as fingerprint unlock not being configured, when it is.
#
# Polkit's fingerprint auth (see etc/pam.d/polkit-1) depends on the same device
# and fails the same way.
#
# The 99 prefix matters. udev applies rules in filename order, and systemd ships
# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-autosuspend.rules, which sets power/control back to
# "auto" for USB devices. At 50 this rule ran first and was overwritten; the
# only reason it appeared to work was that power/control had also been set by
# hand. udevadm test names the winner in its "Written sysfs attributes" summary.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="06cb", ATTR{idProduct}=="00fc", TEST=="power/control", ATTR{power/control}="on"
